n storage stays on SQLite; no SQLite→PostgreSQL migration concern | No migration/consolidation epic; \"application storage unchanged\" is an AC on E002 and E003 |\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Component | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|------|-----------|----------|\n| E001 | Go/no-go decision: PostgreSQL provisioning mechanism for the Gen AI Studio default vector store | Investigation | Gen AI Studio | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n| E002 | Enable and validate the remote pgvector vector_io provider in the OGX distribution | Implementation | OGX Core | OGX Core | P0 |\n| E003 | Wire Gen AI Studio playground OGXServer instances to the remote pgvector vector store | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n| E004 | Validate Gen AI Studio playground RAG on the default pgvector vector store (capability parity, benchmark, sizing) | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n| BRANCH-A-E005 | Operator-based PostgreSQL provisioning for the Gen AI Studio default vector store (branch A: RHAISTRAT-7164 lands in time) | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n| BRANCH-B-E005 | BFF-managed PostgreSQL provisioning for Gen AI Studio playgrounds (branch B: RHAISTRAT-7164 does not land in time) | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n\nBranch epics are conditional alternatives selected by the E001 investigation outcome; they are\nattached to E001 as a plan document, not created as standalone Jira issues.\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[\"E001 Go/no-go: provisioning mechanism (Investigation)\"]\n    E002[\"E002 OGX remote pgvector vector_io provider\"]\n    E003[\"E003 Playground OGXServer pgvector wiring\"]\n    E004[\"E004 Validation: parity, benchmark, sizing\"]\n    BA[\"BRANCH-A-E005 Operator-based PostgreSQL provisioning\"]\n    BB[\"BRANCH-B-E005 BFF-managed PostgreSQL provisioning\"]\n    E002 --> E003\n    E002 --> E004\n    E003 --> E004\n    E001 --> E004\n    E001 -.-> BA\n    E001 -.-> BB\n```\n\nDotted edges are conditional (branch) edges. Arrows point from dependency to dependent.\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E002 → E003 | Rule 12 (framework → consumer) | The gen-ai BFF's OGXServer configuration builds against the distribution's remote pgvector provider support; the provider must be enabled and validated in the distribution first |\n| E002 → E004 | Rule 13 (artifact → consuming implementation) | The validation epic exercises the provider integration it verifies |\n| E003 → E004 | Rule 13 (artifact → consuming implementation) | The validation epic exercises the playground wiring it verifies |\n| E001 → E004 | Rule 3 (Investigation gate, true gate) | The validated target environment depends on the selected provisioning mechanism; `gated_by` with `rewrite` on failure |\n| E001 ⇢ BRANCH-A-E005 | Rule 3 (conditional branch) | Branch A exists only if RHAISTRAT-7164 lands in time for RHOAI 3.5 |\n| E001 ⇢ BRANCH-B-E005 | Rule 3 (conditional branch) | Branch B exists only if RHAISTRAT-7164 does not land in time |\n\n**Non-edges (parallel work), justified:**\n\n| Pair | Rationale |\n|------|-----------|\n| E001 ∥ E002 | The provisioning-mechanism decision and the provider integration resolve different unknowns; the provider work is identical under every outcome |\n| BRANCH-A/B-E005 ∥ E002, E003 | The provisioning mechanism is independent of the provider integration and the BFF wiring; the PostgreSQL connection secret is a configuration reference consumed at runtime — expressed as an AC gate on the consuming epics, never a DAG edge |\n| E003 ∥ BRANCH-B-E005 | Same repo, different areas (BFF vector_io configuration vs. BFF infrastructure provisioning) — merge coordination risk, not a dependency (Rule 15) |\n| No image-build / Konflux-onboarding / repo-onboarding epic | Staff Engineer Correction 2: `registry.redhat.io/rhel9/postgresql-16` already includes pgvector; no custom image, no onboarding chain (Rule 20 does not apply) |\n| No rhods-operator epic | `RELATED_IMAGE_POSTGRESQL_16_IMAGE` is already present in the rhods-operator CSV for disconnected installs — no work required |\n| No docs-authoring epic | No substantive new documentation deliverable is in scope; limitation notes fall out of the validation epic as an acceptance criterion (incidental documentation) |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\nThe full strategy's HLR table was not available (attachment not provisioned); the strategy's\nAcceptance Criteria (AC-1..AC-6) and Staff Engineer directives serve as the requirement set.\n\n| Requirement | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n|-------------|----------|---------|-------|\n| AC-1: Default vector store available out-of-the-box, no manual provisioning/configuration | P0 | BRANCH-A-E005 / BRANCH-B-E005 (selected mechanism), E003 | Mechanism selected by E001; both branches deliver the out-of-the-box instance; E003 removes manual setup from the user path |\n| AC-2: Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function with no loss of current capability | P0 | E002, E003, E004 | Provider integration, wiring, and end-to-end validation |\n| AC-3: Auto Rag functions with the default vector store | P0 | — (removed from scope) | Staff Engineer override: decouple from Auto Rag — deferred to RHAISTRAT-7824 (RHOAI 3.6). Recorded as a scope reduction, not an orphan: the strategy author explicitly removed it |\n| AC-4: Default vector store runs as a separate process, decoupled from the Llama Stack instance | P0 | E002, BRANCH-A-E005 / BRANCH-B-E005 | Remote pgvector provider + separately provisioned PostgreSQL instance |\n| AC-5: Scoped appropriately for multi-tenant environments (per-namespace isolation) | P0 | E003, BRANCH-A-E005 / BRANCH-B-E005 | Per-namespace scoping per Staff Engineer direction (the per-instance driver, Auto Rag, is out of scope) |\n| AC-6: Lightweight enough for resource-constrained environments | P0 | E004, BRANCH-A-E005 / BRANCH-B-E005 | Sizing starts from model-registry-operator reference; adjusted per benchmark (order-of-magnitude parity, no SLA) |\n| SEI-1..SEI-10 (Staff Engineer directives, see override table) | P0 | E001–E004, BRANCH-A/B-E005 | Each directive is traceable to epic scope/ACs as mapped in the override table above |\n\n## Conditional Decomposition Branches\n\nThe E001 investigation has bounded outcomes (≤3) that change which downstream epic exists:\n\n- **Branch A (RHAISTRAT-7164 lands in time for 3.5):** `RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E005.md` —\n  operator-based PostgreSQL provisioning in the RHOAI-controlled operator layer, following the\n  model-registry-operator pattern.\n- **Branch B (RHAISTRAT-7164 does not land in time):** `RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E005.md` —\n  BFF-managed PostgreSQL provisioning (guardrails-infrastructure pattern), with portability to\n  the 3.6 platform-shared database and explicit lifecycle design.\n- **Last-resort fallback (neither branch viable):** bring-your-own PostgreSQL — customers\n  manually configure the OGXServer CR spec with their PostgreSQL connection details. This is\n  documented as the gate's fallback (an AC on E001's decision record), not a branch epic: it\n  requires no implementation work beyond documentation and abandons the out-of-the-box goal.\n\nE004 (validation) depends on the investigation outcome (`gated_by` E001), not on a specific\nbranch, because validation is required under every viable outcome.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n1. **Missing strategy sections (input health):** the full strategy attachment\n   (`RHAISTRAT-8004-strategy.md`) referenced by the Jira description was not provisioned, so\n   the Affected Components table, Impacted Teams table, numbered HLR list, dependencies table,\n   and NFRs were unavailable. Team assignments were derived from component ownership\n   (Gen AI Studio work → Gen AI Studio team; ogx-distribution work → OGX Core team); component\n   names were matched to canonical RHAI components. Verify team assignments against the full\n   strategy when available.\n2. **Schedule risk — hard external dependency:** RHAISTRAT-7164 (dashboard operator\n   modularization, In Progress) gates the preferred operator-based provisioning branch. Per the\n   Staff Engineer Input it will be challenging to commit this strategy to a specific release\n   until that dependency is delivered. The go/no-go gate (E001) manages this; the BFF-managed\n   branch and the BYO fallback bound the worst case.\n3. **Open question resolved by direction, not decision:** the scoping-model open question\n   (per-namespace vs. per-instance) is treated as resolved toward per-namespace because the\n   Staff Engineer Input removes Auto Rag (the per-instance driver) from scope and the fallback\n   provisioning description is per-namespace. The implementing team should confirm per-namespace\n   scoping at E001's decision point.\n4. **Deferred open question — migration path:** whether users need a migration path from the\n   default vector store to a production-grade vector database is not resolved by the Staff\n   Engineer Input. The pgvector choice itself aligns with commonly-used vector database\n   providers (Staff Engineer framing), and the validation epic documents that the default store\n   is for development experimentation. A future migration-path work item should be raised\n   separately if product decides it is needed.\n5. **Priority load:** the strategy is a Blocker (blocks RHAISTRAT-7474), so all epics are P0;\n   the critical path (provider → wiring → validation) is 3 epics long and the go/no-go decision\n   should be made early to avoid serializing the branch work behind it.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable as a classic tiered split: the dependency with uncertain timing\n(RHAISTRAT-7164) does not gate user-facing Tier 1 work — the provider integration (E002),\nplayground wiring (E003), and either provisioning branch each deliver independent progress.\nThe uncertainty is handled by conditional branches