{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-f66fa40a-0945-43be-bad1-676a9e2b8c1f","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-89a91a7932b660f0","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-0adeup6k/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E005.md","content":"# BFF-managed PostgreSQL provisioning for Gen AI Studio playgrounds\n\n## Description\n\nBranch B of the provisioning-mechanism decision: the dashboard operator modularization\n(RHAISTRAT-7164) does not land in time for RHOAI 3.5, so the gen-ai BFF provisions the\nPostgreSQL/pgvector instance backing the default vector store directly — mirroring how the BFF\nalready provisions guardrails infrastructure. When a playground with RAG is configured and no\nsuitable PostgreSQL instance exists in the namespace, the BFF creates the Deployment, Service,\npersistent storage, and credentials Secret for a pgvector-enabled PostgreSQL 16 instance using\nthe existing Red Hat image. Because the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop,\nlifecycle management (cleanup on playground deletion, pod-restart handling, credential\nrotation) receives explicit design attention, and the implementation is portable: when the\nplatform-provided shared database arrives (RHOAI 3.6), the BFF consumes the shared connection\nstring instead of self-provisioning.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Implement BFF-managed provisioning in the gen-ai BFF: when a playground with RAG is\n  configured and no suitable PostgreSQL instance exists in the namespace, create the\n  PostgreSQL 16 (pgvector) Deployment, Service, persistent storage, and credentials Secret,\n  following the existing guardrails-infrastructure provisioning pattern in the BFF.\n- Use the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL 16 image (pgvector included) — no custom image build.\n- Set the provisioned instance's resource requests/limits starting from the\n  model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL sizing.\n- Design and implement lifecycle management explicitly, since the BFF has no reconciliation\n  loop: cleanup of the PostgreSQL resources when the playground is deleted, handling of\n  PostgreSQL pod restarts, and credential rotation.\n- Implement the provisioning behind the same namespaced connection-reference abstraction the\n  playground OGXServer wiring consumes, so that switching to the RHOAI 3.6 platform-provided\n  shared database (or to an operator-provisioned instance if the operator layer lands later)\n  requires no wiring rework.\n- Document the lifecycle design and its limitations relative to operator-based management.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The gen-ai BFF provisions a pgvector-enabled PostgreSQL 16 instance (Deployment, Service,\n  persistent storage, credentials Secret) in the namespace when a playground with RAG is\n  configured and no suitable instance exists, following the BFF's existing\n  guardrails-infrastructure provisioning pattern.\n- The provisioned instance uses the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL 16 image with no custom image\n  build.\n- Resource requests/limits for the provisioned instance start from the model-registry\n  operator's PostgreSQL sizing.\n- Lifecycle behavior is implemented and documented: PostgreSQL resources are cleaned up on\n  playground deletion, pod restarts are handled, and credential rotation is supported.\n- The provisioning is portable: the connection-reference abstraction allows the BFF to consume\n  a platform-provided shared connection string (RHOAI 3.6) instead of self-provisioning,\n  without reworking the playground wiring.\n- No Konflux onboarding, repo onboarding, or custom image build is introduced by this epic.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- Strategy acceptance criterion \"A default vector store is available out-of-the-box on RHOAI\n  without requiring users to manually provision or configure external infrastructure\"\n  (provisioning portion, branch B).\n- Strategy acceptance criterion \"The default vector store runs as a separate process,\n  decoupled from the Llama Stack instance (not an inline provider)\" (separately provisioned\n  PostgreSQL instance).\n- Strategy acceptance criterion \"The default vector store is scoped appropriately for\n  multi-tenant environments (e.g., per-namespace isolation)\" (per-namespace provisioning).\n- Strategy acceptance criterion \"The default vector store is lightweight enough to run in\n  resource-constrained environments without imposing significant overhead\" (reference\n  resource sizing).\n- Staff Engineer Input: \"Alternative fallback — BFF-managed PostgreSQL provisioning\" —\n  guardrails-style provisioning with portability and lifecycle caveats.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":62016,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b24000d35afb1c95","agent_id":"a2f51b2238c42403f","session_id":"d17e1aa1-c63c-47fc-8da1-f24a565f8f93","uuid":"4d640a3b-b4c4-4453-a107-27f87bf1ba97","timestamp":"2026-10-26T13:35:46.145Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8004"}
