{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b17b43e025a3f0f2","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n3\ttitle: 'Go/no-go decision: PostgreSQL provisioning mechanism for the Gen AI Studio\n4\t  default vector store'\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n6\tcomponent: Gen AI Studio\n7\tteam: Gen AI Studio\n8\ttype: Investigation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tdependencies: []\n11\tinvestigation_signals:\n12\t  question_specificity: 1\n13\t  source_accessibility: 0\n14\t  local_runnability: 0\n15\t  cluster_hardware_dependence: 0\n16\t  human_judgment_required: -2\n17\timplementation_type: null\n18\tai_signals: null\n19\tai_implementability: Low\n20\tai_implementability_score: -1\n21\tjira_key: null\n22\tbranch: null\n23\tgated_by: null\n24\tgate_failure_impact: null\n25\t---\n26\t# Go/no-go decision: PostgreSQL provisioning mechanism for the Gen AI Studio default vector store\n27\t\n28\t## Description\n29\t\n30\tThis investigation resolves the single largest open decision in the strategy: how the\n31\tPostgreSQL/pgvector instance backing the Gen AI Studio default vector store is provisioned for\n32\tRHOAI 3.5. The preferred approach — self-provisioning PostgreSQL following the\n33\tmodel-registry-operator pattern — requires a RHOAI-controlled operator for Gen AI Studio,\n34\twhich the dashboard operator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164, In Progress) is expected to\n35\tprovide. If that modularization does not land in time for 3.5, the fallback is BFF-managed\n36\tprovisioning (the gen-ai BFF creates the PostgreSQL resources directly, mirroring how it\n37\tprovisions guardrails infrastructure), with requiring customers to bring their own PostgreSQL\n38\tas the last resort. The outcome of this decision determines which downstream provisioning\n39\timplementation exists, so it must be made before that work is scheduled.\n40\t\n41\t## Scope\n42\t\n43\t- Define the go/no-go criteria for the decision: the RHAISTRAT-7164 delivery milestones that\n44\t  the RHOAI 3.5 release timeline can absorb (cut-over dates), and the readiness conditions the\n45\t  modularized operator layer must meet to carry PostgreSQL lifecycle management for Gen AI\n46\t  Studio.\n47\t- Evaluate the two self-provisioning mechanisms against those criteria:\n48\t  - Operator-based provisioning in the RHOAI-controlled operator layer delivered by the\n49\t    dashboard operator modularization (preferred; follows the model-registry-operator\n50\t    pattern).\n51\t  - BFF-managed provisioning by the gen-ai BFF (fallback; requires explicit lifecycle design\n52\t    because the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop — cleanup on playground\n53\t    deletion, pod-restart handling, credential rotation).\n54\t- Document the last-resort fallback — customers bring their own PostgreSQL and manually\n55\t  configure the OGXServer custom resource spec with their connection details — including its\n56\t  user-facing impact (loss of the out-of-the-box experience) and the conditions under which it\n57\t  would be invoked.\n58\t- Record the decision and its rationale in a decision record reviewed at Gen AI Studio sprint\n59\t  planning, so the selected provisioning implementation can be scheduled.\n60\t- Confirm the vector store scoping model (per-namespace) while making the decision, since the\n61\t  per-instance scoping driver (Auto Rag) is out of scope for this strategy.\n62\t\n63\t## Acceptance Criteria\n64\t\n65\t- A decision record exists that states the selected provisioning mechanism (operator-based or\n66\t  BFF-managed) and the evidence used to select it.\n67\t- The decision record defines the concrete go/no-go criteria (RHAISTRAT-7164 delivery\n68\t  milestones vs. RHOAI 3.5 release dates) and shows the selected outcome satisfies them.\n69\t- The decision record documents the last-resort bring-your-own-PostgreSQL fallback, the\n70\t  conditions that would trigger it, and its user-facing impact.\n71\t- The decision record records the confirmed scoping model for the default vector store.\n72\t- The decision has been reviewed and accepted in Gen AI Studio sprint planning (recorded\n73\t  attendees/date in the decision record).\n74\t\n75\t## HLR Traceability\n76\t\n77\t- Strategy open question \"What is the preferred scoping model — per namespace, per Llama Stack\n78\t  instance, or both?\" — resolved as part of this decision (per-namespace expected; the\n79\t  per-instance driver, Auto Rag, is out of scope per Staff Engineer Input).\n80\t- Staff Engineer Input: \"Operator dependency for self-provisioning (RHAISTRAT-7164)\" — hard\n81\t  dependency and go/no-go gate definition.\n82\t- Staff Engineer Input: \"Fallback if RHAISTRAT-7164 does not land in time\" and \"Alternative\n83\t  fallback — BFF-managed PostgreSQL provisioning\" — both mechanisms and the BYO last resort\n84\t  are evaluated here.\n85\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b90007d49630d841","agent_id":"af41092d649637995","session_id":"d17e1aa1-c63c-47fc-8da1-f24a565f8f93","uuid":"ddb6da89-7e1a-4302-9609-613a7ad9d5a1","timestamp":"2026-10-26T13:37:43.738Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b17b43e025a3f0f2","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
