{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b321b4a90b9f1042","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E005\n3\ttitle: BFF-managed PostgreSQL provisioning for Gen AI Studio playgrounds\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n5\tcomponent: Gen AI Studio\n6\tteam: Gen AI Studio\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tbranch: B\n10\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n11\tdependencies:\n12\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n13\tgate_failure_impact:\n14\t  action: rewrite\n15\t  fallback_approach: If BFF-managed provisioning is judged unacceptable (lifecycle-management\n16\t    risk of a non-operator provisioning path), provisioning falls back to the operator-based\n17\t    approach once the dashboard operator modularization lands, or to the bring-your-own-PostgreSQL\n18\t    last resort (customers manually configure the OGXServer spec with their PostgreSQL\n19\t    connection details).\n20\tai_signals:\n21\t  change_specificity: 1\n22\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n23\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n24\t  existing_foundation: 1\n25\t  open_questions: -1\n26\t  external_dependency: 1\n27\t  human_process_gates: 1\n28\t  repo_access: 1\n29\t  architecture_claims: 1\n30\timplementation_type: null\n31\tinvestigation_signals: null\n32\tai_implementability: High\n33\tai_implementability_score: 7\n34\tjira_key: null\n35\t---\n36\t# BFF-managed PostgreSQL provisioning for Gen AI Studio playgrounds\n37\t\n38\t## Description\n39\t\n40\tBranch B of the provisioning-mechanism decision: the dashboard operator modularization\n41\t(RHAISTRAT-7164) does not land in time for RHOAI 3.5, so the gen-ai BFF provisions the\n42\tPostgreSQL/pgvector instance backing the default vector store directly — mirroring how the BFF\n43\talready provisions guardrails infrastructure. When a playground with RAG is configured and no\n44\tsuitable PostgreSQL instance exists in the namespace, the BFF creates the Deployment, Service,\n45\tpersistent storage, and credentials Secret for a pgvector-enabled PostgreSQL 16 instance using\n46\tthe existing Red Hat image. Because the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop,\n47\tlifecycle management (cleanup on playground deletion, pod-restart handling, credential\n48\trotation) receives explicit design attention, and the implementation is portable: when the\n49\tplatform-provided shared database arrives (RHOAI 3.6), the BFF consumes the shared connection\n50\tstring instead of self-provisioning.\n51\t\n52\t## Scope\n53\t\n54\t- Implement BFF-managed provisioning in the gen-ai BFF: when a playground with RAG is\n55\t  configured and no suitable PostgreSQL instance exists in the namespace, create the\n56\t  PostgreSQL 16 (pgvector) Deployment, Service, persistent storage, and credentials Secret,\n57\t  following the existing guardrails-infrastructure provisioning pattern in the BFF.\n58\t- Use the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL 16 image (pgvector included) — no custom image build.\n59\t- Set the provisioned instance's resource requests/limits starting from the\n60\t  model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL sizing.\n61\t- Design and implement lifecycle management explicitly, since the BFF has no reconciliation\n62\t  loop: cleanup of the PostgreSQL resources when the playground is deleted, handling of\n63\t  PostgreSQL pod restarts, and credential rotation.\n64\t- Implement the provisioning behind the same namespaced connection-reference abstraction the\n65\t  playground OGXServer wiring consumes, so that switching to the RHOAI 3.6 platform-provided\n66\t  shared database (or to an operator-provisioned instance if the operator layer lands later)\n67\t  requires no wiring rework.\n68\t- Document the lifecycle design and its limitations relative to operator-based management.\n69\t\n70\t## Acceptance Criteria\n71\t\n72\t- The gen-ai BFF provisions a pgvector-enabled PostgreSQL 16 instance (Deployment, Service,\n73\t  persistent storage, credentials Secret) in the namespace when a playground with RAG is\n74\t  configured and no suitable instance exists, following the BFF's existing\n75\t  guardrails-infrastructure provisioning pattern.\n76\t- The provisioned instance uses the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL 16 image with no custom image\n77\t  build.\n78\t- Resource requests/limits for the provisioned instance start from the model-registry\n79\t  operator's PostgreSQL sizing.\n80\t- Lifecycle behavior is implemented and documented: PostgreSQL resources are cleaned up on\n81\t  playground deletion, pod restarts are handled, and credential rotation is supported.\n82\t- The provisioning is portable: the connection-reference abstraction allows the BFF to consume\n83\t  a platform-provided shared connection string (RHOAI 3.6) instead of self-provisioning,\n84\t  without reworking the playground wiring.\n85\t- No Konflux onboarding, repo onboarding, or custom image build is introduced by this epic.\n86\t\n87\t## HLR Traceability\n88\t\n89\t- Strategy acceptance criterion \"A default vector store is available out-of-the-box on RHOAI\n90\t  without requiring users to manually provision or configure external infrastructure\"\n91\t  (provisioning portion, branch B).\n92\t- Strategy acceptance criterion \"The default vector store runs as a separate process,\n93\t  decoupled from the Llama Stack instance (not an inline provider)\" (separately provisioned\n94\t  PostgreSQL instance).\n95\t- Strategy acceptance criterion \"The default vector store is scoped appropriately for\n96\t  multi-tenant environments (e.g., per-namespace isolation)\" (per-namespace provisioning).\n97\t- Strategy acceptance criterion \"The default vector store is lightweight enough to run in\n98\t  resource-constrained environments without imposing significant overhead\" (reference\n99\t  resource sizing).\n100\t- Staff Engineer Input: \"Alternative fallback — BFF-managed PostgreSQL provisioning\" —\n101\t  guardrails-style provisioning with portability and lifecycle caveats.\n102\t"}]},"parent