{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aa927932c84f1025","type":"tool_result","content":"=== artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E005-ai-signals.md ===\n# AI Signals — RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E005 (Implementation, branch A)\n\nImplementation signal set (9 signals) — operator-based PostgreSQL provisioning in the\nRHOAI-controlled operator layer (branch A: RHAISTRAT-7164 lands in time for RHOAI 3.5).\n\n| Signal | Value | Rationale |\n|--------|-------|-----------|\n| change_specificity | +1 | The pattern (model-registry-operator self-provisioning), the image (`registry.redhat.io/rhel9/postgresql-16` with pgvector), the disconnected-install mechanism (`RELATED_IMAGE_POSTGRESQL_16_IMAGE`), and the reference resource sizing are all named. |\n| pattern_precedent | +1 | The model-registry-operator already auto-provisions PostgreSQL instances exactly this way (per-instance Deployment, Service, credentials Secret), providing a direct in-platform precedent. |\n| adapter_pattern | +1 | Follows the model-registry-operator's provisioning implementation as the reference pattern. |\n| existing_foundation | 0 | Extends an existing codebase (odh-dashboard / the modularized operator layer), but the RHOAI-controlled operator layer this work must live in is delivered by the in-progress RHAISTRAT-7164 modularization and is not yet present. |\n| open_questions | +1 | Within branch A the mechanism decision is resolved and the design follows an existing pattern; lifecycle behavior (cleanup, rotation) is handled by the operator's reconciliation loop. |\n| external_dependency | -1 | Hard dependency on RHAISTRAT-7164 (dashboard operator modularization, In Progress, another work stream) delivering the operator layer this epic builds in; timing is outside this epic's control. |\n| human_process_gates | 0 | Neutral: no approval board beyond standard review; the release go/no-go was already resolved by the gating decision. |\n| repo_access | +1 | The odh-dashboard repo (dashboard operator) is accessible for clone and modification. |\n| architecture_claims | +1 | `.context/architecture-context/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/model-registry-operator.md` documents the PostgreSQL auto-provisioning pattern this epic follows ({name}-postgres Deployment + credentials Secret, configurable connection parameters), and `ogx-distribution.md` confirms PGVector as a supported backend for the provisioned database. |\n=== artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E005-ai-signals.md ===\n# AI Signals — RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E005 (Implementation, branch B)\n\nImplementation signal set (9 signals) — BFF-managed PostgreSQL provisioning by the gen-ai BFF\n(branch B: RHAISTRAT-7164 does not land in time for RHOAI 3.5).\n\n| Signal | Value | Rationale |\n|--------|-------|-----------|\n| change_specificity | +1 | The resources to create (PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PVC, credentials Secret), the trigger condition (playground with RAG configured, no suitable instance in the namespace), the image, and the reference sizing are all specified. |\n| pattern_precedent | +1 | The gen-ai BFF already provisions guardrails infrastructure the same way, per the Staff Engineer Input — an existing in-codebase precedent for BFF-managed provisioning. |\n| adapter_pattern | +1 | Mirrors the BFF's existing guardrails-infrastructure provisioning pattern. |\n| existing_foundation | +1 | Extends the existing gen-ai BFF (packages/gen-ai/bff in odh-dashboard); no new component required. |\n| open_questions | -1 | The Staff Engineer Input explicitly flags that lifecycle management needs design attention because the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop: cleanup on playground deletion, pod-restart handling, and credential rotation are unresolved design questions for this epic. |\n| external_dependency | +1 | All work is inside the odh-dashboard repo using an existing Red Hat image; no upstream contribution or vendor coordination. |\n| human_process_gates | +1 | No human approval step beyond standard code review. |\n| repo_access | +1 | red-hat-data-services/odh-dashboard is accessible for clone and modification. |\n| architecture_claims | +1 | `.context/architecture-context/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/odh-dashboard.md` corroborates the gen-ai BFF's structure and its role managing playground infrastructure; the guardrails-provisioning precedent and the PostgreSQL/pgvector image facts come from the Staff Engineer Input. |\n=== artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-E001-ai-signals.md ===\n# AI Signals — RHAISTRAT-8004-E001 (Investigation)\n\nInvestigation signal set (5 signals) — the epic resolves the provisioning-mechanism go/no-go\ndecision.\n\n| Signal | Value | Rationale |\n|--------|-------|-----------|\n| question_specificity | +1 | The question is concrete and enumerable: does RHAISTRAT-7164 (dashboard operator modularization, In Progress) deliver a RHOAI-controlled operator layer in time for RHOAI 3.5? Outcomes are bounded (operator-based, BFF-managed, bring-your-own-PostgreSQL last resort) and the selection criteria are definable as dates and readiness conditions. |\n| source_accessibility | 0 | The answer is not yet written down anywhere readable: it depends on the in-progress delivery of another strategy and on release/sprint planning outcomes. No open repo, doc, or public record contains the decision. |\n| local_runnability | 0 | Resolving the decision does not involve exercising any component as a binary or library; it is a planning/roadmap evaluation, not a runtime experiment. |\n| cluster_hardware_dependence | 0 | No gating or peripheral question requires a live cluster, GPU, or production-scale environment; the decision is made from roadmap data and design criteria. |\n| human_judgment_required | -2 | The gating question is itself a human/sprint-planning decision: the Staff Engineer Input explicitly directs the development team to define a go/no-go gate as part of sprint planning. The AI can assemble the criteria and evidence, but the decision requires stakeholder judgment. |\n=== artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-E002-ai-signals.md ===\n# AI Signals — RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 (Implementation)\n\nImplementation signal set (9 signals) — remote pgvector vector_io provider enablement and\nvalidation in the OGX distribution.\n\n| Signal | Value | Rationale |\n|--------|-------|-----------|\n| change_specificity | +1 | The provider id (`remote::pgvector`), its environment guard (`ENABLE_PGVECTOR`), the distribution's config surface, and the target image (`registry.redhat.io/rhel9/postgresql-16` with pgvector) are all named in the Staff Engineer Input. |\n| pattern_precedent | +1 | The distribution already ships remote vector I/O providers (Milvus, PGVector, Qdrant) activated via environment-variable guards; the same provider-plugin pattern is followed. |\n| adapter_pattern | +1 | Follows the distribution's existing provider-plugin reference implementation; the Staff Engineer Input states the existing remote vector store implementation is largely reusable. |\n| existing_foundation | +1 | PGVector is already a supported remote vector I/O backend in the OGX distribution — this extends/configures existing code rather than greenfield work. |\n| open_questions | +1 | All design decisions for this epic are resolved by the Staff Engineer Input: vector_io provider only, application storage untouched, existing image, reuse of the remote implementation. |\n| external_dependency | 0 | The ogx-distribution repo is Red Hat-controlled and the change is expected to be configuration-level; neutral because the extent of pgvector-specific gaps in the reusable implementation is unverified and could require upstream OGX framework coordination. |\n| human_process_gates | +1 | No human approval step is required; standard code review only. |\n| repo_access | +1 | The target repo (red-hat-data-services/ogx-distribution, mirrored from opendatahub-io) is accessible for clone and modification. |\n| architectur