{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b3ce4aff00b93893","type":"tool_result","content":".context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/ai4rag.md\n.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/ogx-distribution.md\n---\n**Detailed**: ogx-distribution packages the OGX framework as a containerized Python service for RHOAI. It acts as a unified AI agent gateway that routes inference requests to configurable remote providers (vLLM, AWS Bedrock, IBM WatsonX, Azure, Vertex AI, OpenAI, Gemini, Anthropic), provides vector I/O through Milvus, PGVector, or Qdrant backends, and offers tool runtime capabilities including web search and file operations. The distribution uses PostgreSQL for both key-value and SQL-backed persistence of agent state, conversations, batches, and metadata. Authentication is handled via OAuth2 token validation with JWKS when configured. The container image is built on an AIPCC base image with Python dependencies installed from the RHEL AI Python Package Index via Hermeto-prefetched packages, and ships pre-fetched Docling and embedding model artifacts. [source: distribution/config.yaml:1-280, Dockerfile.konflux:1-58, distribution/entrypoint.sh:1-79]\nOGX distribution follows a provider-plugin architecture where each API capability (inference, vector I/O, tool runtime, files, batches, responses, messages) is backed by one or more configurable providers. Providers are conditionally activated via environment variable guards (e.g., `${env.VLLM_URL:+vllm-inference}`), meaning the running configuration adapts to the deployment context without code changes. The distribution strips several inline providers (docling, faiss, milvus-inline, sentence-transformers) from the upstream build configuration, shipping only remote-capable providers suitable for production multi-worker deployments. [source: distribution/config.yaml:4-12, build/build.yaml:1-5]\n| **Certificate handling** | System trust store (inherited from UBI base); configurable CA paths for Milvus (`ca_pem_path`, `client_pem_path`) and auth (`tls_cafile`) | distribution/config.yaml:89-91,268 |\n| 2 | OGX Server | Vector DB (Milvus / PGVector / Qdrant) | Configurable | HTTP/gRPC/SQL | Configurable (mTLS for Milvus) | Token / API key / password |\n| Milvus | HTTP client | Optional | Configurable | HTTP/HTTPS | mTLS configurable | Remote vector database for RAG |\n---\n| NeMo Guardrails | varies | HTTPS | TLS | API Key | Content moderation |\n- **External service interactions:** BFF sidecars proxy requests to external platform services: gen-ai BFF connects to Llama Stack servers, MLflow tracking servers, NeMo guardrails, and MCP servers (via SSE/streamable HTTP with `X-MCP-Bearer` authentication); eval-hub BFF queries TrustyAI and KServe InferenceServices; maas BFF communicates with MaaS API servers; and the core-bff proxies to Prometheus/Thanos for metrics dashboards. All external connections use TLS encryption with Bearer token or API key authentication. [source: packages/gen-ai/bff/internal/constants/mcp.go:5-16, packages/eval-hub/bff/internal/api/inferenceservices_handler.go:94]\n| NeMo Guardrails | REST |  | varies | HTTPS | TLS | Content moderation |\n---\n**Short**: Kubernetes operator that manages the lifecycle of ModelRegistry, Catalog, and AIHub custom resources, provisioning model-registry service instances with kube-rbac-proxy authentication, PostgreSQL storage, and Gateway API or OpenShift Route ingress. [source: cmd/modelregistry.go:169, internal/controller/modelregistry_controller.go:282, internal/controller/modelcatalog_controller.go:183]\n**Detailed**: The model-registry-operator is a controller-runtime-based Kubernetes operator that reconciles three CRDs — ModelRegistry (v1alpha1/v1beta1), Catalog (v1alpha1), and AIHub (v1alpha1) — to provision and manage model registry service instances within the RHOAI platform. For each ModelRegistry CR, the operator creates a Deployment running the model-registry proxy backed by either PostgreSQL or MySQL storage, with an optional kube-rbac-proxy sidecar that enforces SubjectAccessReview-based authorization on the HTTPS port. The operator manages the full resource lifecycle including Services, ServiceAccounts, RBAC Roles/Bindings, Secrets, NetworkPolicies, and ingress resources (Gateway API HTTPRoutes or OpenShift Routes depending on cluster capabilities). The ModelCatalog reconciler similarly provisions a centralized catalog service with its own PostgreSQL instance. The operator detects cluster capabilities at startup — including OpenShift APIs, BYOIDC mode (where user.openshift.io is absent), Gateway API availability, and the platform Auth CRD — to conditionally create platform-specific resources. [source: internal/controller/util.go:36-39, internal/controller/config/defaults.go:197-211, internal/controller/config/templates/deployment.yaml.tmpl:220-272]\n| model-catalog-postgres | Controller-created Deployment | postgresql ({image}) |\n| {registry-name}-postgres | Controller-created Deployment | postgres ({image}) |\n| model-catalog-postgres | ClusterIP | 5432/TCP | 5432 | PostgreSQL | Unknown | Unknown | Internal |","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b90007d49630d841","agent_id":"af41092d649637995","session_id":"d17e1aa1-c63c-47fc-8da1-f24a565f8f93","uuid":"6e89244a-e164-44b7-9c0c-ce5588df807d","timestamp":"2026-10-26T13:38:08.164Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b3ce4aff00b93893","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
